@@ -318,3 +318,13 @@ ## Key Learnings (continued)
 - **The "false missing read_products" Shopify bug (2026-08-24) was ALREADY FIXED once, in `ShopifyCapabilityDiagnosticsService` (added earlier, well tested — 9 passing tests in `ShopifyCapabilityDiagnosticsTest.php`) — it just wasn't wired into the primary "Test connection" flow.** Root cause: `ShopifyAuthService::testConnection()` (still used by the OLD Shopify integration settings page and, until this fix, by `ConnectionProfileController::test()` too) hard-gates on `str_contains($token['scope'], 'read_products')` BEFORE ever calling `GET /products.json` — a token whose self-reported `scope` string doesn't literally contain that substring fails the WHOLE connection even when the real endpoint would return 200. Meanwhile `ProductSyncService`/`ProductSyncJob` go through `ConnectorFactory` → `ShopifyConnector` directly and never touch `ShopifyAuthService::testConnection()` at all — which is exactly why "product sync works" while "test connection" reported failure: two completely independent code paths, only one had the bug. Fix: `ConnectionProfileController::test()` now calls `ShopifyCapabilityDiagnosticsService::run()` for `admin_client_credentials` Shopify connections instead of delegating through `IntegrationsController::testConnection()` — real per-endpoint checks (shop/products/orders/locations), scope string is advisory-only (only ever produces a `warning`, never blocks `products.read` from passing). `ShopifyAuthService::testConnection()` itself was left UNCHANGED (still has the old gate, still used by the legacy Shopify settings page/`ShopifyClientCredentialsAuthTest`) — fixing it in one well-tested place and routing the new UI through that, rather than rewriting the old one, was the lower-risk move.
 - **GOTCHA: `ShopifyAuthService::requestToken()` throws (not just warns) when the token's scope string is completely EMPTY** (`trim($scope) === ''`) — this is a SEPARATE gate from the `read_products`-specific one, and it fires before any capability check ever runs (caught by `ShopifyCapabilityDiagnosticsService::run()`'s try/catch → `skippedReport()`, every capability marked `skipped`, nothing actually tested). A test simulating "scope string present but doesn't mention read_products" (the real bug scenario) MUST use a non-empty scope missing only that one entry, e.g. `'write_products,read_orders'` — not `''`, which hits this unrelated gate instead and never reaches the products endpoint at all.
 - **`ConnectionProfileController::authStatus()` now returns `capabilities` (token/shop/products_read/orders_read/inventory_locations, each `ok`/`error`/`skipped`/`unknown`) + `capability_messages` + `warning`, alongside the existing top-level `status`/`checked_at`/`error`.** For Shopify `admin_client_credentials`, these are read back from `settings.diagnostics` (persisted by `ShopifyCapabilityDiagnosticsService::run()`) — never re-runs network calls just to render the page. Top-level `status` is `'connected'` the moment EITHER `shop.read` OR `products.read` passed (matches the "don't fail the whole connection over one endpoint" requirement); a scope-string mismatch on an otherwise-passing `products.read` only sets `warning`, never flips `status` to `'error'`. Editing Shopify credentials (`IntegrationsController::saveShopify`/`saveConnection`) now clears `settings.diagnostics`/`settings.last_token_error`/`metadata.auth_check` (via `collect(...)->except([...])`) so a stale verdict can never survive a credential change — status reads back `needs_setup` until Test connection runs again.
+- **Role-Based Dashboards + Agent Activity Metrics Foundation (2026-08-28):** `agent_activity_events` (append-only ledger, no `updated_at`, `HasUlids`) is written by `App\Services\Activity\AgentActivityRecorder::record()` ONLY when an actor is present — `Order::markAsConfirmed()`/`markAsCancelled()` (WhatsApp customer-reply path) call `OrderWorkflowService::transition()` with `actor: null`, so a customer's own reply never becomes "agent activity" with zero extra branching needed. Hook points, one per event type, always placed AFTER the underlying action already succeeded (never inside a try/catch that could still fail): `OrderWorkflowService::transition()` for confirmation/fulfillment (has no delivery/`OrderShipment` context, so delivery is NOT hooked there); `DispatchService::assign()/markDelivered()/markFailed()` for delivery (branches `delivery.failed` vs `delivery.unreachable` on `$reason === 'customer_unreachable'`); `OrderAssignmentService::claim()/takeNext()` for `confirmation.claimed` (relies on the method's own existing locked-claim guard for dedup — no new idempotency layer); `ReturnInspectionService::disposition()` for `return.inspected`; `InventoryTransferService::receive()` and both `StockController`/`ProductController` `adjustStock()` methods for inventory events. `confirmation.unreachable` and `fulfillment.error_reported` are documented model constants with NO current emitter (no UI action exists for either yet) — don't be surprised these read as permanently-zero in metrics. New `agent_score_rules` table (seeded inline in its own migration's `up()`, no separate seeder) backs `AgentScorePreviewService` — points are computed live every call by joining `agent_activity_events` counts against the rules in-memory; there is deliberately NO `agent_score_events` table, and nothing here is or should ever be wired to payroll (none exists in this codebase). `DashboardController::resolveDashboardKind()` picks one of 6 dashboard kinds from `User::accessProfileForStore($store)['roleSlug']` (owner-tier list, `supervisor`, `confirmation-agent`, `warehouse`→'fulfillment', `dispatcher`→'delivery', `inspector`→'inventory' as a documented judgement call, default 'owner') — same route/page component (`Dashboard/Index.jsx`), which is now a thin router keyed on a `dashboard_kind` prop, each kind rendering its own component from `resources/js/Components/Dashboard/Roles/`. The existing owner dashboard's stats logic was extracted VERBATIM into `OwnerDashboardMetricsService` — zero behavior/visual change for owners. `SupervisorDashboardMetricsService::delayedOrdersCount()` filters on `FulfillmentStatus::phase() IN ['confirmation','fulfillment','delivery']`, NOT `!isTerminal()` — `Completed` is phase `'closed'` but `isTerminal()` returns false for it (its `transitions()` includes `Returned`), so `isTerminal()`-based filtering would wrongly count completed orders as delayed. Test pattern established (mirrors `ConfirmationDeskClaimTest`'s `cdClaimWorkspace`/`cdClaimMember`): `Organization`/`Store`/`StoreMember` workspace via `OrganizationProvisioner::createOwnedOrganization()` + `$store->ensureDefaultRoles()` + `StoreMember::create(['store_role_id' => $store->roles()->where('slug', $roleSlug)->firstOrFail()->id, ...])`; a plain `actingAs($member)->get('/dashboard')` is enough to reach the right dashboard (no manual session store_id needed — `getActiveStore()`'s `accessibleStores()->first()` picks it up), confirmed against the pre-existing `DashboardNavigationVisibilityTest`. Every new Pest test file in `tests/Feature/Foundation/` needs globally-unique helper-function name prefixes (no shared namespace collisions) — used `aae*`/`rbd*`/`adm*`/`sdm*`/`cad*`/`fad*`/`dad*`/`aps*` for the 8 files this phase added.
+- **GOTCHA — `Date::use(CarbonImmutable::class)` in `AppServiceProvider` means `now()` returns `Carbon\CarbonImmutable` app-wide, not `Illuminate\Support\Carbon` (2026-08-28):** any service method type-hinted `Illuminate\Support\Carbon $from`/`$to` throws a `TypeError` the moment a caller passes the `now()`-derived value straight through (e.g. `DashboardController`'s `AgentDashboardMetricsService::rangeFor()` range, or a test's `now()->startOfDay()`). Fix: type-hint date-range parameters as `Carbon\CarbonInterface` (both `Illuminate\Support\Carbon` and `Carbon\CarbonImmutable` implement it) — applied to `AgentDashboardMetricsService`, `AgentScorePreviewService`, `SupervisorDashboardMetricsService`. Model scopes already typed `\DateTimeInterface` (e.g. `AgentActivityEvent::scopeBetween()`) needed no change since `CarbonInterface extends DateTimeInterface`. Any NEW service method taking a date-range argument in this codebase should default to `CarbonInterface`, not a concrete Carbon class.
+- **GOTCHA — Carbon 3's `diffInSeconds()` (and the other `diffIn*` methods) default to a SIGNED result; Carbon 2 defaulted to absolute.** `$end->diffInSeconds($start)` now returns negative when `$end` is after `$start` (this project is on `nesbot/carbon` 3.11). Any duration/elapsed-time calculation written assuming Carbon 2's always-positive default (e.g. `AgentDashboardMetricsService::averageSecondsBetween()`) must wrap the call in `abs()` explicitly. This bug was latent and untested until real Pest coverage exercised it — worth grep'ing for other bare `->diffIn*(` calls if duration bugs show up elsewhere.
+- **GOTCHA — PHP's `json_encode()` drops the decimal point on a whole-number float by default** (`json_encode(80.0)` → `"80"`, which decodes back as `int(80)`, not `float(80.0)`) — `JSON_PRESERVE_ZERO_FRACTION` is required to round-trip it as a float, and Inertia's response encoding doesn't set that flag. Any Pest `assertInertia()->where('some.float_prop', 80.0)` assertion will fail with "80 is identical to 80.0" whenever the real value happens to be a whole number. Fix in the TEST (not the service): compare with a closure, `->where('key', fn ($v) => (float) $v === 80.0)`, rather than asserting an exact float literal.
+- **Owner Dashboard whole-business metrics (2026-08-28):** `OwnerDashboardMetricsService::build()` previously summed `today_sales`/`today_orders`/`month_revenue` from `PosOrder` only — silently POS-only despite the store also having online orders (Shopify/WooCommerce/YouCan/manual, table `orders`). Fixed to combine both channels via new private helpers `posSalesTotal()`/`onlineSalesTotal()`/`posOrdersCount()`/`onlineOrdersCount()` (each takes a `Carbon\CarbonInterface $from/$to` range, not concrete `Illuminate\Support\Carbon` — same `Date::use(CarbonImmutable::class)` reason as the sibling metrics services fixed earlier this session). No double-counting risk exists structurally: a POS sale is never synced into the `orders` table (separate source entirely), so concatenating POS+online recent-orders/totals can never see the same sale twice. Revenue exclusions: `PosOrder.status` has a real `cancelled` value (enum `['completed','cancelled','pending_delivery']`, migration `2026_06_01_000002`) that was previously being counted as revenue — excluded via `POS_REVENUE_EXCLUDED_STATUSES = ['cancelled']`; `pending_delivery` deliberately stays IN revenue (the sale already happened, only delivery is pending — see `OrderPresenter::pos()`'s comment that delivery-vs-instant is a fulfillment concern, never a source/sale concern). `Order.status` (`App\Enums\OrderStatus`) is a PROJECTION of `fulfillment_status`, written by `OrderWorkflowService`/`OrderSyncService`, not an independent field — excluded `Cancelled` (real, produced by the workflow) and `Failed` (dead enum case, never emitted by anything today, excluded defensively) via `ONLINE_REVENUE_EXCLUDED_STATUSES`. Old keys (`today_sales`/`today_orders`/`month_revenue`) kept as the combined totals (frontend `OwnerDashboard.jsx` only reads these + `unpaid_invoices`/`total_products`/`low_stock_count`/`pending_deliveries` — confirmed by reading the component); new additive keys `today_pos_sales`/`today_online_sales`/`month_pos_revenue`/`month_online_revenue`/`today_total_orders` are unused by the frontend today, added only for future callers.
+- **`pending_deliveries` (count) vs `pendingBons` (list) used two DIFFERENT status subsets before this fix** (`['pending','preparing','ready']` vs `['pending','preparing','ready','shipped']`) — unified into one `PENDING_DELIVERY_STATUSES` constant covering all 4 non-terminal `BonDeLivraison.status` values (full enum: `pending, preparing, ready, shipped, delivered, cancelled`, migration `2026_06_01_000009`); `shipped` counts as pending because the customer hasn't received the parcel yet — only `delivered`/`cancelled` are terminal/excluded.
+- **GOTCHA — SQLite (used for this project's Pest test DB) rejects `HAVING` that references a SELECT-list alias when there's no `GROUP BY`, even though MySQL accepts it** (`"HAVING clause on a non-aggregate query"`). `Product::scopeWithSellableStock()` (`app/Models/Product.php`) adds `total_stock`/`damaged_stock` as correlated scalar subqueries (verified via `->toSql()` — NOT a join+group-by), so filtering low-stock products at the DB level via `havingRaw('COALESCE(total_stock,0) <= ?', ...)` needed an explicit `->groupBy('products.id')` added first to satisfy SQLite — harmless since `id` is already unique per row, so grouping changes nothing about which rows match. `->count()` on a query with `groupBy`+`having` was empirically verified (via tinker) to correctly return a scalar count on both engines, not just row-fetch-then-PHP-count. This DB-level filter avoids loading the whole product catalog into memory just to compute `total_products`/`low_stock_count`/`low_stock_products` (previous code did `Product::withSellableStock()->get()` unconditionally, then filtered in PHP) — `total_products` itself needs no `withSellableStock()` at all, a plain `count()` suffices.
+- **Order-action authorization UX (2026-08-28): the "blank 403 page" bug was Inertia's built-in error MODAL, not a full navigation.** `router.post()` on a response with no `X-Inertia` response header (the default for an uncaught `abort_unless(...,403,$msg)`) makes `@inertiajs/core` fire an `invalid` event and, if uncancelled, show its OWN full-screen modal with the raw error HTML in an iframe (`node_modules/@inertiajs/core/dist/index.esm.js`'s `handleNonInertiaResponse()`/`modal_default.show()`) — visually identical to "navigated to a blank page" but not an actual page navigation. Fixed via `bootstrap/app.php`'s `$exceptions->respond()` → `App\Support\InertiaErrorResponder::respond()`, which is intentionally NOT a blanket "convert every 403 into a redirect" — it is gated on `$request->header('X-Inertia') && !$request->isMethod('GET')` (i.e. a genuine SPA action click, never a plain HTTP/Pest request), because ~10+ existing tests across the suite (`ConfirmationDeskClaimTest`, `OrderDepartmentsTest`, `OrderChannelViewsTest`, etc.) assert `->assertForbidden()` on POST routes and would break if the status code ever changed. Only a real Inertia action gets `back()->with('error', $message)`; the exact same route hit without that header keeps the byte-for-byte original 403 response. A full-page GET on 403/404/419/500 instead renders `Inertia::render('Error', [...])->toResponse($request)->setStatusCode($status)` — same trick: status code is ALWAYS preserved, only the response BODY changes, so `assertForbidden()`/`assertNotFound()` (which only check status) never break anywhere in the suite.
+- **GOTCHA: `TestCase::withHeaders([...])` persists across every later request in the same test method — it does NOT scope to one call.** A test doing `$this->withHeaders(['X-Inertia' => 'true'])->post(...)` then a plain `->get(...)` later in the SAME test still carries `X-Inertia: true` on that second call, which trips Inertia's own version-mismatch guard (`Inertia\Middleware::handle()` line ~149: a GET request carrying `X-Inertia` gets a 409 unless `X-Inertia-Version` matches `Inertia::getVersion()`, which a test can't easily pre-compute since the version closure is only registered by the middleware during actual request handling). Fix: pass headers as the per-call 3rd argument instead — `$this->post($uri, $data, ['X-Inertia' => 'true'])` — which does NOT leak into subsequent calls on the same `$this`.
+- **`OrderPresenter::claimState(Order|PosOrder $model, User $user, ?string $assignedUserName)`** is now the single source of `assigned_to`/`assigned_user_name`/`claimed_by_current_user`/`can_claim`/`can_confirm`/`can_cancel` for order-row decoration, mirroring `OrderController::updateStatus()`'s claim gate and `DepartmentController::authorizePhase()` exactly — called from BOTH `OrderController::manage()`'s decorate closure and `DepartmentController::queueFor()`'s (which needed a new `User $user` parameter, all 3 call sites in `confirmation()`/`packing()`/`dispatch()` updated) so the Orders board and every department queue can never disagree about whether a button should be enabled. `can_confirm`/`can_cancel` are only ever true when `fulfillment_status === Pending` — every other phase's transitions are permission-only, exactly as before this change (no claim gate exists outside the confirmation phase today).
